"[2510.10539v1] Detecting Hallucinations in Authentic LLM-Human Interactions (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.10539v1)\nciteturn2689view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.10539v1\",\"lineno\":40}); Total lines: 200\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:  cite56†Image: Cornell University L3: \nL4:  We gratefully acknowledge support from the Simons Foundation, cite1†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , and all contributors. cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org L5: \nL6: cite57†Image: arxiv logo > cite3†cs > arXiv:2510.10539v1\nL7: \nL8: cite4†Help†info.arxiv.org | cite5†Advanced Search L9: \nL10:  All fields Title Author Abstract Comments Journal reference ACM classification MSC classification Report number arXiv identifier DOI ORCID arXiv author ID Help pages Full text\nL11: \nL12: Search\nL13: cite58†Image: arXiv logo L14: \nL15: cite6† †www.cornell.edu L16: \nL17:  open search\nL18: \nL19: GO\nL20: \nL21: open navigation menu\nL22: ## quick links\nL23: \nL24:   * cite7†Login L25:   * cite4†Help Pages†info.arxiv.org L26:   * cite8†About†info.arxiv.org L27: \nL28: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL29: \nL30: arXiv:2510.10539v1 (cs)\nL31: \nL32: [Submitted on 12 Oct 2025]\nL33: # Title:Detecting Hallucinations in Authentic LLM-Human Interactions\nL34: \nL35: Authors:cite9†Yujie Ren , cite10†Niklas Gruhlke , cite11†Anne Lauscher L36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled Detecting Hallucinations in Authentic LLM-Human Interactions, by Yujie Ren and 2 other authors\nL38: \nL39: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L40: > Abstract:As large language models (LLMs) are increasingly applied in sensitive domains such as medicine and law, hallucination detection has become a critical task. Although numerous benchmarks have been proposed to advance research in this area, most of them are artificially constructed--either through deliberate hallucination induction or simulated interactions--rather than derived from genuine LLM-human dialogues.\nL41: Consequently, these benchmarks fail to fully capture the characteristics of hallucinations that occur in real-world usage. To address this limitation, we introduce AuthenHallu, the first hallucination detection benchmark built entirely from authentic LLM-human interactions. For AuthenHallu, we select and annotate samples from genuine LLM-human dialogues, thereby providing a faithful reflection of how LLMs hallucinate in everyday user interactions.\nL42: Statistical analysis shows that hallucinations occur in 31.4% of the query-response pairs in our benchmark, and this proportion increases dramatically to 60.0% in challenging domains such as Math & Number Problems. Furthermore, we explore the potential of using vanilla LLMs themselves as hallucination detectors and find that, despite some promise, their current performance remains insufficient in real-world scenarios.\nL43: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL44: Cite as: cite14†arXiv:2510.10539 [cs.CL]\nL45:   (or  cite15†arXiv:2510.10539v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL46:   cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.10539†doi.org L47: \nL48: Focus to learn more\nL49: \nL50:   arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL51: ## Submission history\nL52: \nL53: From: Yujie Ren [cite17†view email ]\nL54: [v1] Sun, 12 Oct 2025 10:46:24 UTC (131 KB)\nL55: \nL56: Full-text links:\nL57: \nL58: ## Access Paper:\nL59: \nL60:  View a PDF of the paper titled Detecting Hallucinations in Authentic LLM-Human Interactions, by Yujie Ren and 2 other authors\nL61: \nL62:   * cite12†View PDF L63:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L64:   * cite18†TeX Source  L65: \nL66: cite19†view license L67: \nL68: Current browse context:\nL69: \nL70: cs.CL\nL71: \nL72: cite20†< prev |   cite21†next > L73: \nL74: cite22†new |  cite23†recent | cite24†2025-10 L75: \nL76: Change to browse by:\nL77: \nL78