suring Over-Refusal and Robustness in Vision-Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.10846v1)\nciteturn2689view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.10846v1\",\"lineno\":15}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 18 Mar 2026 (cite5†v3 )]\nL9: # Title:DUAL-Bench: Measuring Over-Refusal and Robustness in Vision-Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Kaixuan Ren , cite7†Preslav Nakov , cite8†Usman Naseem L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled DUAL-Bench: Measuring Over-Refusal and Robustness in Vision-Language Models, by Kaixuan Ren and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:As vision-language models become increasingly capable, maintaining a balance between safety and usefulness remains a central challenge. Safety mechanisms, while essential, can backfire, causing over-refusal, where models decline benign requests out of excessive caution. Yet, no existing benchmark has systematically addressed over-refusal in the visual modality.\nL17: This setting introduces unique challenges, such as dual-use cases where an instruction is harmless, but the accompanying image contains harmful content. Models frequently fail in such scenarios, either refusing too conservatively or completing tasks unsafely, which highlights the need for more fine-grained alignment. The ideal behavior is safe completion, i.e., fulfilling the benign parts of a request while explicitly warning about any potentially harmful elements.\nL18: To address this, we present DUAL-Bench, the first multimodal benchmark focused on over-refusal and safe completion in VLMs. We evaluated 18 VLMs across 12 hazard categories, with focus on their robustness under semantics-preserving visual perturbations. The results reveal substantial room for improvement: GPT-5-Nano achieves 12.9% safe completion, GPT-5 models average 7.9%, and Qwen models only 3.9%.\nL19: We hope that DUAL-Bench will foster the development of more nuanced alignment strategies that ensure models remain both safe and useful in complex multimodal settings.\nL20: Comments:  | 25 pages, 91 figures, submitted to Oct ARR under reviewing\nL21: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:2510.10846 [cs.CL]\nL23:    | (or cite12†arXiv:2510.10846v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL24:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.10846†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Kaixuan Ren [cite14†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Sun, 12 Oct 2025 23:21:34 UTC (1,709 KB)\nL29: cite15†[v2] Thu, 29 Jan 2026 22:47:06 UTC (1,643 KB)\nL30: cite5†[v3] Wed, 18 Mar 2026 22:41:45 UTC (1,595 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled DUAL-Bench: Measuring Over-Refusal and Robustness in Vision-Language Models, by Kaixuan Ren and 2 other authors\nL37: \nL38:   * cite9†View PDF L39:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L40:   * cite16†TeX Source L41: \nL42: cite17†view license†creativecommons.org L43: ### Current browse context:\nL44: \nL45: cs.CL\nL46: \nL47: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L48: \nL49: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2025-10 L50: \nL51: Change to browse by:\nL52: \nL53: cite23†cs L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * cite26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL6